    "turn_text": "have an interesting journey, and that ties us all together. My father didn't become a pastor until later in life. So when I was a child, he was in business. He was very involved in the church, but he didn't become a full-time pastor until I was an adult. So I didn't have the experiences of many pks of every Sunday getting ready for the Sunday sermon. That was not my experience growing up. But I grew up in the church and faith was and is integral to our family. You know, I've shared a number of times my personal testimony, and for me, it's a family journey. It didn't start with me as I shared last night when I was three. My parents and I were living up in Calgary, and neither of my parents were believers, and both of them drank far too much. And my father decided to leave us. So he left my mother and me, and went back to Texas, went back to Houston. And he decided he didn't have time to be a father anymore. He didn't want to be a husband. He wanted to be on his own. And he was gone for a number of months. And when he was down in Houston, a colleague of his from the Olympic Gas Business invited him to a Bible study. And he went And he is described being amazed and very puzzled by the peace that everyone had at that Bible study. Everyone was facing challenges. Nobody's life was easy. One woman that he remembers to this day was a woman who described how her son would beat her to get money to buy drugs. And yet she still had this peace, what scripture calls a peace that passes understanding, that he couldn't understand. It made no sense to him. How could she have that peace with such a terrible home life? And he began asking questions and asking more and more questions. And the couple who's home, the Bible study was being held at. They said, well, our pastor's coming tomorrow night. Why don't you come back and ask him your questions? And he said, okay. And so he came back the next night and he spent four or five hours arguing with the pastor. And my father was a young man. He was very, very bright. He was a scientist. He was an atheist. And he was convinced he knew everything. He was convinced that religion couldn't possibly be true. And so he argued with the pastor until about 11 o'clock at night. Then he asked, he said, well, what about the man into bed? Who's never heard of Jesus? And the pastor, his name was Galen Wiley. He was the pastor of Clay Road Baptist Church, which was a small church in Houston. And Brother Wiley very wisely didn't take that big. Or he would have been there another four or five hours arguing. And Steady said, Raphael, I don't know about the man in Tibet. But you've heard of Jesus. What's your excuse? And my dad has described how that just hit him like a ton of bricks. And he dropped to his knees and he gave his life to Jesus. And it turned my father around. The next Sunday he went to Clay Road Baptist Church, James baptized. And then he drove to the airport and bought a ticket and flew back to Calgary and rejoined my mother and me. And so a lot of people wonder whether faith is real. I can say in my family, if it were not for the transformative love of Jesus Christ, I would have been raised by a single mom without my dad in the house. And shortly thereafter, the three of us moved down Houston. And that next year my mother was saved as well. And so as a child I grew up in the church. And then when I was eight, I went to a church summer camp at Clay Road, along with my cousin, BB, who's five years older than I am. And it's like a sister to me. And both of us became Christians at that summer camp. I was baptized that summer. And it transformed our lives. I describe one of the amazing things about the campaign is you travel around the country. visit with people all over the place and we had a big rally on Murfreesboro, Tennessee. A couple thousand people came out and unbeknownst to me, Galen Wiley had retired to Tennessee and was living there and he came to the rally and I found out just a few minutes before the rally started, someone told me he was going to be there and I asked him to come forward and I hadn't seen him in 35 years. The last time I'd seen him I was probably 10 years old. And it was an amazing and powerful moment simply to embrace him, say thank you. Thank you for sharing the Gospel on that night, April 15th, 1975 in Houston, Texas, that it transformed not only my father's life, but my mother's life and my life and the life of my wife, Heidi, and our children. I mean, in terms of the the ripples of a pond of the generations that are impacted by sharing",
